Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md. I judged the diff and checked the two spawn-site claims with grep; I didn't run the binary, since build and tests had already passed.

What the change does. There is nowhere in yoyo to add --no-config: the model's search runs rg from inside yoagent, so yoyo never builds that command. Instead, while a fence is active and the search root is a directory, the result filter now drops any line before yoagent's footer that doesn't name a file under the searched root. Dropped lines get their own count. The rule lives inside fence_tool_result, which both guarded tool wrappers already send every result through, as the placement note asked.

Spawn sites, confirmed. src/ has no Command::new("rg"). /grep and yoyo grep run grep (in commands_search.rs), so a ripgrep config never reaches them. The other "rg" strings in src/ (tools.rs, rtk.rs) are lookup tables, not places where rg gets started.

Tests. They cover each output shape the probe found leaking (--no-filename, --null, --json, --path-separator, and -C1 context lines). With no fence, and for ordinary results outside the denied directory, output is checked to be byte-identical with assert_eq!. A separate test confirms that a single-file root is not put into the drop-everything mode.

Docs and commit message. permissions.md and ARCHITECTURE.md are both updated. The commit body records the probe results and lists the rest of #1023 as still open: the stale (N matches) footer and warning lines that name a denied path.

Trade-off: if a user has a ripgrep config that reshapes output and a fence is active, allowed matches are dropped too. This is documented, along with the remedy: unset RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH.
